"In Lesson 2 you saw that a picture is a long list of numbers, and that a photo from a phone holds millions of them. Yet the photo files on a phone are often far smaller than that. Songs and videos are smaller than you would expect too. Something is making them shrink. It is called data compression: reducing the amount of data needed to store a piece of information or to send it. The first idea is repetition. Suppose a row of a picture has sixteen squares, all of them red. You could write “red” sixteen times. Or you could write “16 red.” That is two numbers instead of sixteen, and nothing has been lost. A picture with large areas of one color, such as a blue sky above green grass, shrinks a great deal this way. But it does not always work. Take a picture where no two squares next to each other are the same color. Now every square has to be written as “1 of this color,” which is two numbers instead of one. The file gets bigger, not smaller. Compression is not magic. It finds patterns, and where there is no pattern, there is nothing to save. Compression of this kind is called lossless. When the file is unpacked, every number comes back exactly as it was. That matters for text, where every character is important, and for programs. ZIP files work this way, with cleverer methods than counting. English text can generally be compressed to one-half or one-third of its original size. For photos and music there is a second kind, called lossy compression. It throws away detail that people hardly notice. The JPEG format for photos discards what the human eye cannot see. The MP3 format for music discards sounds that the ear is least likely to miss. The files become very much smaller. But the detail that was thrown away is gone, and no program can bring it back. So there is a choice to make. An essay must come back letter for letter, so it needs lossless compression. A photo to share with friends can lose a little. Either way, a smaller file takes up less storage and travels more quickly to another computer. And a file that has already been compressed, such as a JPEG photo, will hardly shrink at all if you compress it again.": "24f0bb20d161",
